steps.body:
`for { }` with no condition at all loops forever. You end it from the inside with `break`, which jumps to the first line after the loop.

This shape is useful when the exit test belongs in the middle or at the end of the body. Go has no do-while, so this is how you write one: the body runs once before the check.

```go
for {
	// work that must happen at least once
	if done {
		break
	}
}
```

Every `for { }` needs a `break` that is sure to run, or the program hangs. As the for-loops lesson showed, `break` inside a `switch` only leaves the `switch`. Here that is worse than a skipped exit: if the only `break` sits in a `switch`, the loop never ends. Write the exit test as an `if`.

Loops often track the largest value seen so far. It is the default-then-override pattern from the ternary lesson, repeated on every pass. Start `maxVal` at the first value, then compare each new one:

```go
if n > maxVal {
	maxVal = n
}
```

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 6
	steps := 0
	maxVal := n
	for {
		fmt.Print(n)
		if n == 1 {
			break
		}
		fmt.Print(" -> ")
		if n%2 == 0 {
			n /= 2
		} else {
			n = 3*n + 1
		}
		steps++
		if n > maxVal {
			maxVal = n
		}
	}
	fmt.Println()
	fmt.Printf("Steps: %d\n", steps)
	fmt.Printf("Max: %d\n", maxVal)
}

